; Phalanx - the flying fortress (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN A7, roster type EIGHT).
;
; THE ESCALATION ANCHOR. P4.6 says a pressured enemy fields heavier things rather
; than more things, and until this landed `HEAVY_TYPES` was [aegis, screamer] -
; a bomber that correctly leaves defensive garrisons (A.q1) and a jammer with no
; weapon. So "the war escalates" meant "the war gets foggier" and nothing else.
;
; THESE NUMBERS ARE THE SECOND DRAFT, and the first flight is why. The user built
; it in their head as a capital ship and got a gunboat: "i imagined the Phalanx
; way more massive, like a battle ship... its turrets are too small to
; individually take down, i find the Phalanx to die before any of its turrets die
; individually. its should be like a flying fortress."
;
; The mount-stripping arc was never being SEEN, and the reason was arithmetic
; rather than logic. At 140 hull and 3 mounts of 30, the whole body was 230 points
; against a blaster that lands about 275 a second - the entire fight, guns and
; all, was over inside a second. Nothing was broken; there was simply no time for
; a sequence to read as a sequence.
;
;   hull 140 -> 700, mount_hull 30 -> 100, mount_count 3 -> 6.
;
; Six mounts of 100 sit in front of a 700 hull, so there are 1300 points to work
; through and the guns come off one at a time, on the bearings you choose, while
; the volume of return fire visibly falls. The scene grew to match - a 6 x 2 x 8 m
; hull with a tower and a keel, guns out on the beam at 3.4 m - because "too small
; to individually take down" was as much about SIZE as about hull.
;
; THE ARC WRAPS PAST THE BEAM, also the user's steering: "the arc can also be more
; than a half sphere, it can encapsulate beyond that, leaving a tighter hold at
; the opposite side where the shield is aimed at, making it even more
; challenging." So 250 degrees, leaving a 110 degree window ASTERN of the facing.
;
;   shield_arc_deg 140 -> 250, shield_slew_deg_s 45 -> 28.
;
; THE SLEW HAD TO COME DOWN WITH THE ARC, and that pairing was measured rather
; than guessed. The screen now tracks the threat it can SEE (the bug the user
; found on the same flight), so the only way to land damage is to out-turn it and
; hold the lead. Flying a 30 m orbit at 25 m/s - 47.7 deg/s of bearing change:
;
;   arc 250, slew 28  ->  opens at 1.8 s, 3.5 s windows, shut 65% of the time
;   arc 250, slew 45  ->  opens at 12.7 s. Too long to be a fight.
;   arc 290, slew 45  ->  NEVER opens. An enemy no pilot can hurt.
;
; That last row is why `phalanx_check` flies this exact orbit and asserts the
; window exists: the failure is a property of the PAIR, and no assertion on either
; number alone could catch it.
;
; damage 5 x fire_rate 0.8 x 6 mounts = 24 damage a second at full strength,
; falling as you strip it. It is deliberately close to the first draft's output
; despite doubling the gun count: the fight is now three times longer, and holding
; the old damage rate over the new duration would have made the type a wall rather
; than a siege.
;
; speed 5 / accel 4 / turn_speed_deg 55 - it is a capital ship. `turn_speed_deg`
; is the MOUNT slew here, not the body's, which is what a jinking pilot degrades.
;
; strength_cost 10 makes it far and away the most expensive unit in the manifest -
; a node buys ONE of these instead of ten raiders. That is P4.6's escalation
; expressed as a price rather than as a spawn rule.
;
; NOTHING HERE IS BALANCED. Per H6 these are authored starting points; the harness
; measures the result and the human's hands decide the feel.
;
; ONE THING LAYER 1 CANNOT SEE, stated so nobody quotes it wrong: `Lethality`
; models the shield as a plain pool that always applies, because that is what
; `Health` does. That is exactly right for a pilot inside the arc and pessimistic
; for one outside it, so Layer 1 prices the WORST case. The gap between that and
; the harness is the directional shield, and it is the instrument's output rather
; than an error.

[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="EnemyConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/enemy_config.gd" id="1_ecfg"]

